---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PolicySection from "../components/Privacy/PolicySection.astro";

const lastUpdated = "November 2026";

const shortVersion = [
  {
    claim: "No cookies.",
    aside: "Not even the edible kind. We checked the whole site twice.",
  },
  {
    claim: "No tracking.",
    aside:
      "Which also means we have no idea whether anyone reads this. Hi, if you do.",
  },
  {
    claim: "No ads.",
    aside: "Nobody is bidding on your attention here.",
  },
  {
    claim: "No sign-ups.",
    aside: "There’s nothing to register for. One less password to forget.",
  },
];

const principles = [
  {
    title: "Only what the feature needs",
    body: "If a feature can work without your data, it does. If it can’t, it gets the minimum and nothing “just in case”.",
  },
  {
    title: "Private by default",
    body: "What you create stays yours. Sharing is something you choose, never something you have to switch off.",
  },
  {
    title: "No ads, no trackers, no selling",
    body: "Our business is building good software, not knowing what you had for breakfast.",
  },
  {
    title: "Said plainly",
    body: "If we collect something, we tell you what and why, in words a human would use.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy — digitowly"
  description="How digitowly.com handles your data, and how we think about privacy in our apps. Short version: as little as possible."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="gutter relative overflow-hidden bg-paper pt-36 pb-16 sm:pt-44 sm:pb-24">
    <div
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="pointer-events-none absolute -top-[25vw] -left-[30vw] size-[80vw] rounded-full bg-mist/70 blur-3xl"
    ></div>
    <p class="rise relative text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] text-moss uppercase">
      Privacy
    </p>
    <h1
      class="display rise relative mt-6 text-huge text-balance"
      style="animation-delay: 120ms"
    >
      Your data is{" "}
      <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal text-moss">yours.</span>
      We’d like to keep it that way.
    </h1>
    <p
      class="rise relative mt-10 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/75"
      style="animation-delay: 260ms"
    >
      What happens to your data when you visit this website. The short version
      fits on a postcard. The long version exists because the law likes
      paragraphs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Short version -->
  <section class="gutter bg-sage py-20 text-moss sm:py-32">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] uppercase">
      The short version
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-10">
      {shortVersion.map(({ claim, aside }) => (
        <li class="flex flex-col gap-2 border-t border-moss/25 py-7 sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:justify-between sm:gap-10">
          <span class="display text-big">{claim}</span>
          <span class="serif-accent max-w-md text-xl leading-snug text-moss/80 sm:text-right sm:text-2xl">
            {aside}
          </span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Our approach -->
  <section class="gutter bg-sand py-20 sm:py-32">
    <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] text-moss uppercase">
      Our approach, in general
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.2fr] lg:gap-20">
      <h2 class="display text-big text-balance">
        Privacy is part of{" "}
        <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal">good UX</span>. Not a
        checkbox next to it.
      </h2>
      <div class="max-w-xl space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink/80">
        <p>
          We think software should feel calm. That’s hard to pull off while it’s
          quietly reporting your every tap to a dozen companies in the
          background. Cookie banners and “we value your privacy” pop-ups are a
          bad experience on their own, before you even get to the data part.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some things can’t work without any data, and we won’t pretend
          otherwise. But “some” is doing a lot of work in that sentence. Here’s
          what we stick to in everything we build:
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ol class="mt-16 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-10 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      {principles.map(({ title, body }, i) => (
        <li class="border-t border-ink/15 pt-6">
          <span class="serif-accent text-2xl text-moss">
            {String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
          </span>
          <h3 class="display mt-3 text-2xl text-balance sm:text-3xl">
            {title}
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/70">{body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3 class="display mt-24 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">
      What that means{" "}
      <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal">in practice.</span>
    </h3>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <article class="flex flex-col rounded-[2rem] bg-forest p-7 text-cream sm:p-10">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] text-amber uppercase">
          Skauz
        </p>
        <h4 class="display mt-4 text-3xl text-balance sm:text-4xl">
          Needs a little.{" "}
          <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal text-amber">
            Exactly that little.
          </span>
        </h4>
        <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-cream/80">
          A wildlife map without locations is just a list of animals. So Skauz
          does need some data, and only this:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-cream/80">
          <li>
            An account, so your sightings have an owner: your email, a nickname
            and a profile picture from your login.
          </li>
          <li>The sightings you log, including where you saw them.</li>
          <li>
            Your location, only if your browser asks and you say yes, to show
            what’s around you.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-cream/80">
          Sightings are private until you decide to share them. No ads, no
          trackers, and nobody else gets to know where the rare owl lives.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-auto pt-8 text-sm text-cream/60">
          Skauz is still in early development and will get its own detailed
          privacy policy.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="flex flex-col rounded-[2rem] bg-mint p-7 text-pine sm:p-10">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] text-teal uppercase">
          Rhymate
        </p>
        <h4 class="display mt-4 text-3xl text-balance sm:text-4xl">
          Needs almost nothing.{" "}
          <span class="serif-accent tracking-normal text-teal">
            Your lyrics stay yours.
          </span>
        </h4>
        <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-pine/80">
          No account, no analytics, no ads. Your songs live on your devices and
          in your own iCloud. When you look up a rhyme or a definition, only
          that single word is sent to the dictionary services. Never your
          lyrics. Your half-finished breakup song is safe.
        </p>
        <a
          href="/rhymate/privacy"
          class="group mt-auto inline-flex items-center gap-2 pt-8 font-medium text-teal"
        >
          Rhymate privacy details
          <span
            aria-hidden="true"
            class="transition-transform duration-500 ease-calm group-hover:translate-x-1"
          >
            →
          </span>
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Long version -->
  <section class="gutter bg-paper py-20 sm:py-32">
    <div class="mb-6 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-end sm:justify-between">
      <h2 class="display text-big">The long version</h2>
      <p class="serif-accent text-xl text-ink/60">
        For this website. Last updated {lastUpdated}.
      </p>
    </div>

    <PolicySection no="01" title="Who is responsible">
      <p>
        The controller responsible for data processing on this website within
        the meaning of the GDPR is:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>digitowly</strong> · Robert Pasdziernik
        <br />
        Düsseldorfer Straße 159, 51063 Köln, Germany
        <br />
        <a href="mailto:info@digitowly.com">info@digitowly.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        More details are in the <a href="/impressum">Impressum</a>. If you have
        any questions about privacy, just write. A real human will answer.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="02" title="Visiting this website">
      <p>
        This website is hosted by <strong>Netlify, Inc.</strong> (San Francisco,
        USA). Like any web server, theirs needs some technical information to
        deliver a page to you. When you visit, the following is processed
        automatically and may be stored in server log files for a short time:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your IP address</li>
        <li>date and time of the request</li>
        <li>the page requested and the referring page</li>
        <li>browser type, version and operating system</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This data is used solely to deliver the website and keep it secure and
        stable. It is not combined with other data and not used to build
        profiles. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest
        in running a working, secure website. Netlify may process this data in
        the USA. More information is in{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Netlify's privacy policy
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="03" title="Cookies, analytics & fonts">
      <p>
        This website does <strong>not</strong> use cookies, analytics, tracking
        pixels, social media plugins or advertising. There is nothing to consent
        to, which is why you didn't have to dismiss a banner to read this.
      </p>
      <p>
        All fonts are hosted on our own server. Your browser doesn't contact
        Google Fonts or any other third-party service when it loads this site.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="04" title="Contacting us">
      <p>
        If you send us an email, we use your address and whatever you write to
        answer you and, if needed, follow up. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b)
        GDPR if your message is about a project or contract, otherwise Art.
        6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest in replying to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        We delete the conversation once it's done, unless legal retention
        periods (for example for invoices) require us to keep it longer.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="05" title="Your rights">
      <p>Under the GDPR, you have the right to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>access the personal data we hold about you (Art. 15)</li>
        <li>have it corrected (Art. 16) or deleted (Art. 17)</li>
        <li>restrict its processing (Art. 18)</li>
        <li>receive it in a portable format (Art. 20)</li>
        <li>object to processing based on legitimate interest (Art. 21)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To use any of these, email{" "}
        <a href="mailto:info@digitowly.com">info@digitowly.com</a>. Given how
        little we store, it'll be a short email.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory
        authority. For us, that is the Landesbeauftragte für Datenschutz und
        Informationsfreiheit Nordrhein-Westfalen (
        <a
          href="https://www.ldi.nrw.de/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          ldi.nrw.de
        </a>
        ).
      </p>
    </PolicySection>

    <PolicySection no="06" title="Changes">
      <p>
        If we change how this website handles data, we'll update this page. The
        “last updated” date above tells you when that last happened.
      </p>
    </PolicySection>
  </section>
</Layout>
